// EXAMPLE FILE, not compiled (note the .dm.example extension, not .dm).
// This shows how to add a brand new tail sprite. The same pattern works
// for any other character customization sprite (horns, wings, ears,
// snout, etc.), just under a different type path.
//
// How to add your own tail:
//
// 1. Add your tail's icon_states to a .dmi file under modular_zzmeta/icons/,
//    for example modular_zzmeta/icons/customization/tails.dmi (create the
//    folders if they don't exist yet). See tails.dmi.example next to this
//    file for a real tail (the game's otter tail) set up exactly the way
//    yours should be before running the wag-splitting tool described below.
// 2. Copy this file to
//    modular_zzmeta/code/modules/customization/sprite_accessories/tails.dm
//    and add it to tgstation.dme, alphabetically, in the modular_zzmeta
//    section.
// 3. Uncomment the type below and fill in your own name/icon_state/icon.
// 4. Compile, then check your tail shows up when customizing a character.
//
// ///datum/sprite_accessory/tails/mammal/wagging/my_new_tail
//     name = "My New Tail"
//     icon_state = "my_new_tail"
//     icon = 'modular_zzmeta/icons/customization/tails.dmi'
//
// A few things worth knowing:
//
// - If your tail is only meant for a specific species, subtype under an
//   existing species branch instead of wagging directly, e.g.
//   /datum/sprite_accessory/tails/mammal/wagging/vulpkanin/my_new_tail, or
//   add recommended_species = list(...) yourself.
// - If your tail's colors should follow the player's fur/scale colors,
//   you don't need to do anything, that's the default. If it should only
//   take one color, add color_src = USE_ONE_COLOR. If it shouldn't be
//   recolored at all, add color_src = null.
// - See code/datums/sprite_accessories.dm for other vars you can set, like
//   fluffy, or dimension_x/center if your icon isn't the usual 32x32.
//
// Making your tail wag at adjustable, mood-scaled speed (optional):
//
// If your tail has an animated "wagging" sprite baked into the .dmi (the
// usual tgstation-style multi-frame icon_state), you can hook it up to
// this project's tail wag speed system so players can slow it down, speed
// it up, and (optionally) have it scale with their mood, instead of it
// always playing at one fixed speed.
//
// All that's required is that your animated icon_state's name contains the
// word "wagging" somewhere in it, which it already will if you built it the
// normal way described above. Then run:
//
//     tools/bootstrap/python modular_zzmeta/tools/tail_wag_split/split_wag_frames.py modular_zzmeta/icons/customization/tails.dmi
//
// on your .dmi file. It finds your animated wagging state and splits it
// into individual frames the game can step through at a variable speed.
// See modular_zzmeta/tools/tail_wag_split/README.md for more. If you skip
// this step, your tail will still wag, just always at its original baked
// speed.
